{"step_index":86,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-10T11:07:31Z","input_tokens":5759,"cache_read_tokens":220777,"output_tokens":17713,"thinking":"Initial assessment: The user has provided a binary search implementation. The core logic hinges on repeatedly dividing a sorted array in half. The `mid` calculation and comparison with the `target` are the key elements. The code appears standard, but it's crucial to examine the comparison and loop termination conditions for correctness.\n\n\nThe \"left is not defined\" error likely stems from the JavaScript engine's inability to handle Python's tuple unpacking (`left, right = ...`). The JavaScript engine was likely invoked instead of the Python backend due to an incorrect direct call or, possibly, due to a lack of support for multiple assignment or standalone function calls in the JavaScript implementation.\n\n\nThe primary issue is the JavaScript engine's limitations in handling Python-specific DSA code, especially tuple assignments and top-level function calls. The JavaScript engine needs modification to correctly interpret these constructs. Ensuring both Python and JavaScript engines can handle common DSA patterns seamlessly is the main goal. This requires robust support for multiple assignment, variable swapping, and automatic invocation of user-defined functions or class methods.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"// engine.js — Python AST Support Checker & Step-by-Step Execution Engine\n// Supports DSA patterns: tuple unpacking (`left, right = 0, len(arr) - 1`), array swaps,\n// list item assignments (`arr[i] = x`), top-level functions (`def binary_search(arr, target):`),\n// and `class Solution:` methods.\n\nconst PRELOADED_PROGRAMS = {\n  max_subarray: {\n    title: \"1. Maximum Subarray (Kadane's Algorithm — Default)\",\n    defaultInput: \"nums = [-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4]\",\n    algorithmOverview:\n      \"This code finds the largest sum you can get from a continuous section of the list.\",\n    code: `class Solution:\n    def maxSubArray(self, nums: list[int]) -> int:\n        c = 0\n        ans = nums[0]\n\n        for i in range(len(nums)):\n            c += nums[i]\n\n            if c >= ans:\n                ans = c\n\n            if c < 0:\n                c = 0\n\n        return ans`\n  },\n  binary_search: {\n    title: \"2. Binary Search (Two Pointers: left, right, mid)\",\n    defaultInput: \"arr = [-7, -6, -5, 2, 3, 4, 5], target = 4\",\n    algorithmOverview:\n      \"This code searches a sorted list for a target number by repeatedly checking the middle index (`mid`) and cutting the search range (`left` to `right`) in half.\",\n    code: `def binary_search(arr, target):\n    left, right = 0, len(arr) - 1\n\n    while left <= right:\n        mid = (left + right) // 2\n        if arr[mid] == target:\n            return mid\n        elif arr[mid] < target:\n            left = mid + 1\n        else:\n            right = mid - 1\n\n    return -1`\n  },\n  nested_loops: {\n    title: \"3. Nested Loops: Pair Sum Counter\",\n    defaultInput: \"nums = [1, 2, 3, 4]\",\n    algorithmOverview:\n      \"This code checks every pair of numbers in the list using two nested loops and counts how many pairs add up to an even number.\",\n    code: `pairs = 0\ntotal = 0\n\nfor i in range(len(nums)):\n    for j in range(i + 1, len(nums)):\n        pair_sum = nums[i] + nums[j]\n        if pair_sum % 2 == 0:\n            pairs += 1\n            total += pair_sum\n\nprint(\"Even pairs:\", pairs)\nprint(\"Total sum:\", total)`\n  },\n  recursion_factorial: {\n    title: \"4. Recursion & Functions: Factorial\",\n    defaultInput: \"n = 4\",\n    algorithmOverview:\n      \"This code calculates the factorial of `n` (4 * 3 * 2 * 1 = 24) by having the function call itself with `k - 1`.\",\n    code: `def factorial(k: int) -> int:\n    if k <= 1:\n        return 1\n    sub = factorial(k - 1)\n    return k * sub\n\nans = factorial(n)\nprint(\"Factorial is:\", ans)`\n  }\n};\n\nclass PythonExecutionEngine {\n  constructor() {\n    this.maxSteps = 400;\n  }\n\n  validateSupport(sourceCode) {\n    if (!sourceCode || !sourceCode.trim()) {\n      return {\n        ok: false,\n        line: 1,\n        message: \"The code editor is empty. Paste or write a Python program to analyze.\"\n      };\n    }\n\n    const lines = sourceCode.replace(/\\r\\n/g, \"\\n\").split(\"\\n\");\n\n    const unsupportedRules = [\n      { regex: /^\\s*(import|from)\\s+/, msg: \"`import` statements are not supported in the offline sandbox.\" },\n      { regex: /^\\s*async\\s+/, msg: \"`async` / `await` constructs are not supported.\" },\n      { regex: /\\bawait\\s+/, msg: \"`await` expressions are not supported.\" },\n      { regex: /^\\s*with\\s+/, msg: \"`with` context managers and file I/O are not supported.\" },\n      { regex: /\\byield\\b/, msg: \"Generator `yield` statements are not supported.\" },\n      { regex: /\\blambda\\b/, msg: \"`lambda` expressions are not supported; please use `def`.\" },\n      { regex: /^\\s*(try|except|finally)\\b/, msg: \"`try / except` blocks are not supported.\" },\n      { regex: /\\b(open|eval|exec|__import__|input)\\s*\\(/, msg: \"Restricted built-in function (`open`, `eval`, `exec`, `input`) is disabled for security.\" }\n    ];\n\n    let parenDepth = 0;\n    let bracketDepth = 0;\n\n    for (let i = 0; i < lines.length; i++) {\n      const raw = lines[i];\n      const stripped = this.stripInlineComment(raw);\n      const trimmed = stripped.trim();\n      if (!trimmed) continue;\n\n      for (const rule of unsupportedRules) {\n        if (rule.regex.test(trimmed)) {\n          return {\n            ok: false,\n            line: i + 1,\n            message: `Unsupported Syntax on Line ${i + 1}: ${rule.msg}`\n          };\n        }\n      }\n\n      if (/^(class|def|if|elif|else|for|while)\\b/.test(trimmed) && !trimmed.endsWith(\":\")) {\n        retur